// TRIPWIRE MINE charge — tear the wall out and throw it.
//
// Runs from `tripmine_bore`, 12 px inside the wall the mine was mounted on.
// `shatter` converts that rock into loose rigid fragments and launches each
// one radially away from THIS point, with the speed falling off toward the
// rim. Seated inside the wall, "radially away" and "out into the corridor,
// along the beam, at whoever broke it" are the same direction — fragments
// on the far side of the centre are launched deeper into solid rock and
// just settle. That burial is the entire aiming mechanism; nothing in this
// weapon ever asks where the worm is.
//
// `include_rock` because the walls worth mounting a mine on are usually the
// indestructible kind.
//
// **`cell_size: 6` and `speed: 20` are measured, not taste** (see
// crates/webnoita-core/tests/tripmine_scratch.rs). One big slab cannot be
// thrown, for three compounding reasons: the fragment sitting ON the launch
// point has `d = pos - centre ≈ 0`, so it gets no launch DIRECTION at any
// speed; the fragments are born packed into the socket the shatter just cut
// and have to solve their way out of it; and the velocity pass is an
// approximate projector (SOLVER-MATH-AUDIT.md F5) that eats what survives.
// Measured: a 109-cell slab at `speed: 16` travels 7 px in 90 ticks. At this
// pitch the tear instead throws ~24 fist-sized chunks, of which ~7 (≈167
// cells of wall, biggest ≈44) cross a worm 48 px down the beam within a
// second. The wall arrives; it just arrives as a volley.
//
// `speed: 20` is the launch inside the socket, not the travel speed — what
// gets out runs at ~1-2 px/frame. Worth watching on thin-walled maps
// anyway: bodies have no CCD (F4), and fast debris can ratify its way
// through a 1-2 px partition.
//
// `repel` is NOT what throws the chunks — the blast impulse is mass-scaled
// against a 16-cell reference, so anything slab-sized barely notices it. It
// is here for the loose rubble and to shove whoever is standing at the wall.
//
// **The spall** (2026-08-06) is the other half of the charge, and the fast
// half. Everything above describes rock that needs the better part of a
// second to cross the gap; `tripmine_shrapnel` crosses it in a tenth of
// that. It is also the one thing here that is AIMED — not radially, but at
// `angle_offset: 180` off the bore's own heading, which is the beam, which
// is where the trespasser is standing. That the bore has a heading at all
// is recent and deliberate (see particles/tripmine_bore.json5); before it
// did, this emit would have fired due east out of every mine on the map.
//
// The cone is born inside the socket the shatter just cut, `offset: 8` out
// along each fragment's own line, and most of it dies there against the
// fresh debris — which is the mechanism, not a loss. A fragment stopped by
// a chunk hands that chunk a directed impulse along the beam, so the volley
// spends itself buying exactly what this weapon has always been trying to
// deliver: wall, moving at the worm, sooner and straighter than a radial
// launch inside a packed socket manages on its own. What threads the gaps
// arrives ahead of the rock and arrives as damage.
//
// **Measured** (crates/webnoita-core/examples/tripmine_spall_probe.rs, which
// runs the shipped defs end to end — thrown mine, grace, beam, trespasser),
// worm 50 px down the beam from a wall-mounted mine:
//
//   | arm                        | wall past the worm | worm 100 -> |
//   | no volley (before this)    | 153 cells, plateau at t+30 | 100 |
//   | volley, shove+damage off   | 153 cells, plateau at t+30 | 100 |
//   | shipped                    | 232 by t+30, 307 by t+60   |  52 |
//
// The middle row is the control that matters: identical emission, identical
// RNG draws, only the impulse and the damage switched off. It lands exactly
// on the no-volley curve, so the doubling in the third row is the shove and
// nothing else. All 24 fragments leave the wall (mean velocity -7.8 px/frame
// along the beam); about one in nine reaches the worm.
//
// **Two things to know before retuning the damage.** First, the weapon's
// only damage at range IS this volley — the `damage` band below is r 20
// around a charge seated in the wall, so before the spall a trespasser
// standing in the open at 50 px took literally nothing and just watched the
// rubble come. Second, one trip detonates the charge THREE times (beams
// already in flight when the chisel removes the casing each run their own
// chisel home), so the volley a worm actually eats is 3 x 24 and the numbers
// below are for the whole event:
//
//   gap from the wall |  12   25   40   55   75  100 px
//   damage taken      | 168   78   63   60   24   12
//
// Hugging the wall is fatal, mid-corridor is most of a worm, and the far end
// of the tripwire's own 120 px reach is a scratch. If that triple ever
// becomes a single, this cone is suddenly a third of what it says here.
{
  "do": [
    { "shatter": { "r": 16, "cell_size": 6, "speed": 20, "include_rock": true } },
    { "emit": { "type": "tripmine_shrapnel", "count": 24,
                "angle_offset": 180, "spread": 66, "offset": 8 } },
    { "repel": { "r": 20, "force": 0.06 } },
    { "damage": { "r": 20, "amount": 26 } },
    { "stain": { "r": [0, 26], "strength": 0.35, "texture": "nuke_scar" } },
    { "modify_temperature": { "r": [0, 10], "amount": 25, "limit": 150 } }
  ],
  "presentation": {
    "sounds": { "start": "exp4.wav" },
    "sprite_wl": { "start": 40, "frames": 15, "delay": 1 },
    "sprite": "s40_15",
    "lit": false,
    "shadow": true,
    "shake": 4,
    "flash": 1,
    "light": { "rgb": [1, 0.72, 0.36], "intensity": 1.4, "radius": 48 }
  }
}
